out.push(SAMPLE(['Four paragraphs, and the fourth is the one almost everybody wastes in exactly the same way. Lubna read hers — in conclusion, a full explanation is better than an assumption — and then read her first paragraph: explaining fully is better than assuming the other person understands. Asked what the reader had gained from paragraph four, she said nothing: she had told them where she started and called it where she finished. So the rule. A conclusion must do two things — follow from the paragraphs, and add one thing that was in none of them. Only one, and not a new argument, because a new argument needs its own evidence; one consequence, one limit, or one question the three paragraphs have made askable. Jad asked how that differs from a new argument, and Ms Huda said the thing you add needs no evidence because the paragraphs have already paid for it. Then Dima asked about "on balance", and the answer is that it commits you to having weighed something, so it is only honest after a concession: concede nothing in paragraph two, write "on balance" in paragraph four, and you have used a weighing word with nothing in the scales. Omar asked whether the examiner reads the last sentence properly, and Ms Huda said they read the first and the last most carefully of all, and read them together, because that pair tells them whether the essay went anywhere — forty words, and the whole of "develops the topic" decided by them. Which is why line four of the plan exists: you write what the conclusion adds before you write paragraph two. Then Rim gave hers. Her paragraph three has the two replies in it; what is not in it is that she will now never know what the other eighty-eight letters would have done, because she did not put a figure in them, and she cannot go back. Ms Huda said that is a conclusion: it follows, it adds something nobody could have said before reading the paragraph, and it costs the writer something.']));
out.push(A('Listener task: the student\'s first and last sentences written side by side, with a verdict. ⚠ This is the single most useful exercise in the unit and it takes forty seconds: write the two sentences next to each other with nothing between them, and the question answers itself.'));
