out.push(SAMPLE(['Forty minutes, and the division Ms Huda gives every class, because the paper is not won by writing better than the person next to you but by not running out of time in paragraph three. Four minutes reading the question and underlining the two notes. Four on the four lines. Twenty-four on paragraphs one to three. Four on paragraph four. Four reading it back. Jad asked whether four minutes is really needed just to read the question, and the answer is that most of it goes on one thing — which note am I conceding? — because a candidate who starts writing before that is decided writes paragraph two twice. And the notes are underlined on the paper and not mentally, because an essay that omits one of them loses content marks whatever else is right about it. Then Jad said he had counted his from last week: two hundred and fifty-one words, with a conclusion of one sentence that is paragraph one again. Which is what always happens. A 250-word essay is not a longer essay; it is an essay with no conclusion. The band is 180 to 220 and the top of it is a warning and not a target: at 220 you have had time to read it back, and at 250 you have taken the conclusion\'s four minutes and spent them in paragraph two. Hiba asked whether it is marked down for length, and Ms Huda said it is marked down for what the length did to it, which is worse, because the candidate thinks they have been punished for being thorough. Then the last four minutes, and the four checks in order: both notes present, a concession with a limit, one example and not three, and the first and last sentences read together. Nothing else — not spelling, not vocabulary, not making it nicer. And the repair rule: make the smallest repair that fixes it, and never rewrite a paragraph in the last four minutes, because an unfinished repair costs more than the fault did. And Adnan asked what to do about being nine words over, and Ms Huda said leave them: nine words over is nothing and counting them costs a minute you need. The length matters at the ends — under a hundred and sixty or over two hundred and forty and the examiner notices — and between those it is the four checks that decide the mark, which is why the last four minutes are spent on the checks and not on the count.']));
out.push(A('Listener task: the division of the forty minutes from memory, and the student\'s own line two. ⚠ Have the division written from memory and not copied. Students who cannot produce the five figures will not use them under pressure, and the whole value of the cycle is that the figures are available in minute one of the paper.'));
