out.push(S('Stage 4 · Your turn (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '_**Marked for Hiba, 3.6 essay.** Content: both notes, own idea present → passes. Communicative achievement: position in one is "predictability rather than speed"; in four it is "who is allowed to say the number is wrong" — so it has moved, and it moved by weighing, because paragraph two concedes ten days and names the colleague who was right → passes well. Organisation: four paragraphs, and the conclusion adds the question of authority → passes. Language: four shapes, three errors → good range, and the errors are in the shapes she is reaching for, which is the right way round. **And the one good thing: she names the person who was right about the ten days, in her own essay, and then separates what she was right about from what was done in her name.**_',
  '_**And my own 1.7 essay, marked on the same four.** Content: one note of two → fails. Communicative achievement: no position in either paragraph → fails. Organisation: three paragraphs, no conclusion → fails. Language: nine sentences, two shapes, one error. **One thing it does that my 3.6 essay does not: it has a real figure in the second line — thirty-one empty boxes — and it uses it to say something nobody told me to say, which is that the box was mine. My 3.6 essay has the same figure and a better shape, and it does not say that.**_',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report – mediation (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Four descriptors, and the class marked each other before marking themselves. Dima said the second is the hardest: content she can count, organisation she can count, language she can see, but whether it reads like an essay is an opinion. Ms Huda said it is the hardest and it is not an opinion, and gave the question that makes it countable — does the writer hold the same position in paragraph four as in paragraph one, and did they get there by weighing rather than by repeating? A writer who concedes nothing and then writes "on balance" has failed descriptor two, and you can point at the word. Omar said so descriptor two is really about the concession, and at B2 it very largely is. Then the first-evening essays came out. Jad\'s is dated the eighth of September and is one paragraph. Hiba\'s has four paragraphs and no position — "there are advantages and disadvantages" in the first line and again in the last. Nour\'s concedes nothing at all, and she said she did not know there was anything to concede. Mazen\'s has three examples in one paragraph and none in the other two. So they marked them honestly on the four, and then said one thing each did well, because there always is one. Lubna\'s: content, one note of the two, so it fails; organisation, three paragraphs; language, accurate throughout and all of one shape. And the thing it does well — it has a figure in it. Thirty millimetres, in the second line, on the eighth of September, before anybody had taught her anything. Which is what Ms Huda wanted the cycle to leave them with, and it is not the failure: every one of those essays has one thing in it that four weeks of teaching did not put there. Jad\'s is one paragraph and the last sentence of it is the only genuinely third point in the room. Hiba\'s concedes nothing and is the best-organised page of the twelve. Adnan said his is four lines long and that he did not know what an essay was. And now he does, and he has the four lines. So the honest arithmetic of the unit: what they have gained in four weeks is a shape and a concession. What they have not gained is a position — Lubna had one on the eighth of September and did not know it was one. The shape is teachable in four weeks; having something to say was already there. And the warning that goes with it: a candidate who learns the shape and brings nothing to put in it writes a perfectly organised essay with nothing in it, which is descriptors one and two gone.']));
out.push(A('Listener task: the one thing the 1.7 essay does that the 3.6 essay does not. ⚠ Collect every answer. This is the most important list the unit produces, and it is almost always the same kind of thing: **a figure, a name or an admission that the taught shape squeezed out.** Read six of them aloud in a row before saying anything, because the pattern is the teaching.'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**✗** Marking the writer against each other instead of the essay against the descriptors. → **✓** four descriptors, one essay at a time. An essay that fails content and concedes well is more use to its author than one that passes content and weighs nothing.   **✗** Counting errors before shapes. → **✓** shapes first. Descriptor four is range **and then** accuracy, in that order.   **✗** Putting the one good thing first. → **✓** last. First it is a preamble; last it is the sentence they remember.'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read – exam practice (B2 First Reading and Use of English Part 1)'));
out.push(A('**1** B _(make a mistake)_   **2** C _(pays attention)_   **3** D _(bear in mind)_   **4** A _(weighed the question)_   **5** C _(tell the reader that)_   **6** B _(take the argument)_   **7** D _(objected to it)_   **8** A _(on balance)_'));
out.push(A('Two things to say about this task, because it is the first Part 1 of the course. **Part 1 is a test of collocation and not of meaning**: in item 3 all four words mean roughly the same and only _bear_ goes with _in mind_, and a candidate who reasons about the meaning will take twice as long and get it wrong. ⚠ And item **7** is the kind that decides the paper: the four verbs differ only in the **preposition they take** — _argued with_, _opposed_ (no preposition), _refused_ (no preposition), _objected to_ — and the gap is followed by _to_. **Read the three words after the gap before you look at the options.** That one habit is worth more in Part 1 than any amount of vocabulary.'));
out.push(S('Stage 6 · Write with help (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**MARKED FOR JAD** · A. Barghouti · 9 October',
  '**1 Content.** Note one _(the cost of reprinting)_: present, paragraph two. Note two _(the passengers)_: present, paragraph three. Own idea: the drivers stopped trusting it first. → **Passes.**',
  '**2 Communicative achievement.** Position in paragraph one: _trust rather than accuracy_. In paragraph four: _who is allowed to write on it_. It has moved. Concession: the money, conceded outright in paragraph two with _on the money they are right_. _On balance_: used, and earned. → **Passes well.**',
  '**3 Organisation.** Four paragraphs. First and last read together: _trust rather than accuracy_ → _who is allowed to write on it_. Goes somewhere. → **Passes.**',
  '**4 Language.** Five shapes _(cleft, concessive pair, _take the case of_, a reduced relative, a final rhetorical question)_, four errors, three of them articles. → **Good range; the accuracy is where I would spend the next fortnight.**',
  '**And the one thing it does well.** It concedes the exact ground it had been fighting on for two years, in six words — _on the money, they are right_ — and then shows that the money was never the question. I have been drafting paragraphs for nineteen years and I have never once done that.',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 7 · Write freely – exam practice (B2 First Writing Part 1: the essay)'));
out.push(A('⚠ **This is the same task as 1.7 and the 1.7 essay is on the desk.** The instruction not to look at it until the new one is finished is load-bearing: a student who reads the old one first writes a correction of it rather than an essay, and the comparison is then worthless. **Both are kept.**'));
out.push(A('**Mark the new essay on the four descriptors and nothing else, and do not mark the old one again** — it has been marked once, in Stage 4, by its author. ⚠ The comparison belongs to the student and not to the teacher: the useful sentence is the one each of them writes in the margin, and a teacher who writes it for them has taken the only thing the four weeks produced.'));
out.push(S('Unit 3 check · A  Grammar and vocabulary'));
out.push(A('**1** a   **2** c   **3** b   **4** a   **5** b   **6** c   **7** b   **8** a'));
out.push(A('Item **1** is the agreement trap: _patience_ sits between the subject and the verb. Item **2** is word order — _it is true that_ and not _truly it is_. Item **6**: only _but_ carries the contrast, and _and_ turns the sentence into a list of two things nobody is asking. Item **7** is the declared word-formation set.'));
out.push(S('Unit 3 check · B  Listening (B2 First Listening Part 3 style – multiple matching)'));
out.push(A('**Speaker 1** C   **Speaker 2** A   **Speaker 3** D   **Speaker 4** H   **Speaker 5** G'));
out.push(A('The three unused letters are **B**, **E** and **F**. ⚠ Part 3 is won and lost on the **distractors**, and this set is built to show how: Speaker 4 mentions his conclusion, which pulls towards **B**, and the option that actually matches him is about **time** and not about the conclusion. Teach the habit of reading all eight options before the recording starts, and of crossing out a letter only when it has been used — because the three extras are chosen to be things somebody in the recording very nearly says.'));
out.push(S('Unit 3 check · C  Writing'));
out.push(A('The essay, 180–220 words, in forty minutes with a clock, and the **five timings in the margin**. ⚠ Mark it on the four descriptors and nothing else, and look first at the timings: a student whose plan took eight minutes will have a short paragraph three, and a student whose paragraph three took fourteen will have no conclusion. The timings diagnose the fault faster than the essay does.'));
out.push(S('I can… (Unit 3)'));
out.push(A('Self-assessment; no answers. The four that matter are the first, the fourth, the sixth and the seventh: a sentence somebody could disagree with and be wrong about, a conceded point with a limit, **one** example that could have gone the other way, and a conclusion that is not paragraph one again. A class that ticks the four lines and the _rarely raised_ phrase and not those four has learnt a shape and will fill it with nothing.'));
out.push(box('Looking ahead to Unit 4', [
  'Unit 3 was about holding a position. **Unit 4** is about saying how sure you are — in **nine steps instead of three** — and about the difference between what you know and what you have worked out.',
  '⚠ **4.3 teaches B2 Speaking Part 2 and it is the first time the course asks a student to speak for a full minute without interruption.** Time it from the first lesson. A candidate who has never been timed talks for thirty-five seconds and stops, and the thirty-five seconds cannot be fixed on the day.',
  '**Ask the class now for two photographs each** — a telephone is fine — of **the same thing at two different times**: a form before and after a change, a room, a shelf, a street, a noticeboard. 4.3 runs on the students\' own pairs and not on supplied ones, because the whole point of Part 2 is comparing two things you can see and speculating about what you cannot.',
  '**And ask for the paragraph each of them rewrote in 2.7.** 4.6 separates what is known from what is inferred, and that paragraph is the first place most of them will find they have been mixing the two.',
], C.lorange, C.orange));

